      <article class="toc-content prose prose--wide">

        <p style="font-size:1.1rem;line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:2.5rem;padding:1.5rem 1.75rem;background:var(--bg-alt);border-left:3px solid var(--gold-500);border-radius:0 var(--radius-sm) var(--radius-sm) 0">
          The Service Site Standard is a set of seven commitments about how I do the work, every time. If you've read the technical approach, the service agreement, or the owner's guide, you've already seen it at work; this page gives it a name.
        </p>

        <h2 id="one-owner">1. One owner, end to end</h2>
        <p>The person who answers the phone is the person who writes the code, and you keep that same contact for as long as we work together.</p>
        <p><strong>What this means in practice:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Every email comes straight to me.</li>
          <li>One person handles the call, the design, the build, the launch, and the support afterward.</li>
          <li>If the contact form sends something odd at 8 p.m., I'm the one who replies.</li>
          <li>Nobody else works on your project.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="custom-code">2. Custom code over CMSes</h2>
        <p>A CMS (content management system) is the editing platform most websites run on. Your site doesn't need one: I write every page from scratch, so it stays fast as it grows and gives attackers no login page to target.</p>
        <p><strong>What this means in practice:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>No plugin updates, security patches, database backups, or admin logins to worry about.</li>
          <li>Your site works the same in 2030 as in 2026, because the technology underneath it stays put.</li>
          <li>Your domain stays in your name, and if you ever leave, I help you move it to the registrar you choose.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="posted-prices">3. Posted prices, posted process</h2>
        <p>Pricing, the service agreement, and the owner's guide are all public, so you can read everything about working together before we talk. That keeps the call short and starts us on the same page.</p>
        <p><strong>What this means in practice:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="/pricing/" class="inline-link">Pricing</a> shows the numbers: one plan, $175 a month, $0 down.</li>
          <li><a href="/agreement/" class="inline-link">The service agreement</a> is published in plain English for you to read before you sign.</li>
          <li><a href="/owners-guide/" class="inline-link">The owner's guide</a> describes what happens after launch.</li>
          <li>My written checklist covers every step, from the first call to the day we stop working together.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="fast-by-construction">4. Fast by construction</h2>
        <p>A score of 95 to 100 on Google's speed test comes from how the site is built, not from a fix added later. Images come in several sizes and modern formats, and each page carries very little code. Speed pays: Google's research found that when a phone page takes three seconds to load instead of one, a visitor is 32% more likely to leave.</p>
        <p><strong>What this means in practice:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Every demo site scores 95&ndash;100 on phones, in real visitor sessions as well as in testing.</li>
          <li>On a phone, pages are ready to use in under 1.5 seconds.</li>
          <li>The visitor's browser picks the smallest image that fits the screen.</li>
          <li>I check speed every time I publish a change.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="accessible-by-default">5. Accessible by default</h2>
        <p>I hold every site to the WCAG 2.2 AA accessibility standard <em>before launch</em>, and the agreement says so. I check color contrast, keyboard use, visible focus outlines, and page structure, and I test with a screen reader before sign-off.</p>
        <p><strong>What this means in practice:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Text is easy to read against its background, in light mode and dark mode.</li>
          <li>Buttons and links are large enough to tap easily.</li>
          <li>Every page has a "skip to content" link, headings in order, and a visible outline that shows where you are.</li>
          <li>The <a href="/accessibility/" class="inline-link">accessibility statement</a> is a promise backed by the agreement.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="local-before-global">6. Local before global</h2>
        <p>Every site is built to help you show up first in Google's map results (the "local pack"). For most service businesses, the Google Business Profile is the most valuable free source of visitors, and your website's job is to support it. I also teach every client a 15-minute Business Profile checklist to run each quarter.</p>
        <p><strong>What this means in practice:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Labels in each page tell Google what kind of business you are and what you offer, matched to your Business Profile.</li>
          <li>Your name, address, and phone number (often called "NAP") appear the same way everywhere.</li>
          <li>City pages show photos of work in <em>that</em> city, written for that area.</li>
          <li>Service-area maps use an open map service that sets no cookies.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="boring-infrastructure">7. Boring infrastructure</h2>
        <p>I choose proven, unfashionable tools and skip whatever is trending. Everything under your site has been stable for years, so your site keeps working for years too.</p>
        <p><strong>What this means in practice:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>The site you launch in 2026 still works in 2030, without a forced move to new software.</li>
          <li>A typical client site relies on fewer than 20 outside software packages, each locked to a tested version.</li>
          <li>If something breaks at 9 p.m., there are few places for a problem to hide.</li>
          <li>The <a href="/technical-approach/" class="inline-link">technical approach</a> names every tool and explains why it's there.</li>
        </ul>

        <hr style="border:0;border-top:1px solid var(--line);margin:3rem 0">

        <h2 id="how-this-shows-up">How the Standard shows up across the site</h2>
        <p>Every public page here reflects the Standard:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>The <a href="/owners-guide/" class="inline-link">owner's guide</a></strong> puts one owner and the posted process into plain language for every client.</li>
          <li><strong>The <a href="/agreement/" class="inline-link">service agreement</a></strong> puts the posted process in writing.</li>
          <li><strong>The <a href="/samples/" class="inline-link">six demo sites</a></strong> show custom code and speed by construction, and you can test any of them yourself.</li>
          <li><strong>My internal checklist</strong> applies the same steps to every project.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>A page that serves none of the seven principles doesn't belong on this site. That's the test the Standard applies to itself.</p>

